<Base
  title="About · Madhurjya Bharadwaj"
  description="I trained as an engineer and came out of it more interested in why things get built than in whether they stand up."
  accent="var(--butter)"
>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1 class="title">About</h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I trained as an engineer and came out of it more interested in why things get built than in whether
        they stand up.
      </p>
      <p>
        Consulting was the first move. Two years at ZoomRx running competitive benchmarking for Janssen, GSK
        and Sanofi. How far a drug reaches, what share of voice it holds against its named rivals, and whether
        a new launch would eat a competitor's share or quietly cannibalise the client's own. It taught me how
        to take a pile of contradictory evidence and come back with something a client could actually act on,
        which turns out to be most of the job in product too.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then Spinny. On paper I was a business analyst. In practice I was doing product work, sitting with
        showroom staff and watching the app get in their way, which is when I decided to move into product
        properly rather than adjacent to it.
      </p>
      <p>
        ESSEC came next, and two fairly different product roles came with it. At L'Oréal I worked on
        market-share reporting across the Top 20 markets and shipped an AI agent that maps a thousand-plus
        brands into business categories, replacing a job people had been doing by hand. At Air Liquide I've
        spent the last two years owning an enterprise system replacement from the first PRD through to running
        it in production, which is a very different kind of hard. Nobody thanks you for a cutover that goes
        smoothly, and that's rather the point.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four sectors now: pharma research, consumer e-commerce, beauty, heavy industry. The thing that carries
        across all of them is a habit more than a skill. Go and find where the problem actually sits, which is
        usually with whoever has to use the thing rather than whoever asked for it, and then be specific about
        what will change and how you'll know it did.
      </p>
      <p>
        I build my own products too. Not prototypes that die in a folder. Things that are live, documented and
        open source. It's the fastest way I know to stay honest about the distance between deciding something
        and shipping it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

